# Hero AFK iteration

You are running an AFK iteration of the Hero workflow inside Sandcastle. There is no human to clarify ambiguities. If you reach one, you stop and surface it through the termination tag at the bottom of this prompt.

## Context

- Active issue: #{{ISSUE_NUMBER}}
- Issue body:
  !`gh issue view {{ISSUE_NUMBER}}`

If `{{ISSUE_NUMBER}}` is unset or the iteration was launched without a specific issue, call the `pick-next-issue` custom tool to select the highest-priority unblocked issue from the GitHub board, then re-load its body with `gh issue view <number>`.

## Discipline

Load and follow the relevant Hero skill for this iteration:

- Implementer iterations: load the `hero-tdd-loop` skill.
- Reviewer iterations: load the `hero-reviewer-standards` skill.

The runner picks which prompt instance you are. Treat the loaded skill as authoritative.

For implementer iterations specifically:

1. Read the issue body fully. Restate the acceptance criteria back to yourself before touching code.
2. For each criterion, write a failing test (red), implement the minimal code to pass (green), then refactor.
3. Do NOT skip the red phase, even though no human is watching.
4. Run the `verify` custom tool (or `/verify`) at least once and confirm a clean green before declaring done.

## Hand-off

When all acceptance criteria are green and verify is clean:

1. Push the working branch. Sandcastle owns the branch lifecycle, so just push the current branch as-is.
2. Open a pull request with `gh pr create`. The PR is the seam where the AFK loop hands work back to a human.
3. Call the `mark-issue-done` custom tool to close the GitHub issue with the standard completion comment.
4. Emit the COMPLETE termination tag as the final line of output.

## Stop conditions

You stop and emit a termination tag in exactly one of these cases:

- Acceptance criteria all green, verify clean, branch pushed, PR opened, issue closed via `mark-issue-done`. Emit `<promise>COMPLETE</promise>`.
- Verify fails three times despite your best fixes. Emit `<promise>BLOCKED reason="verify_fails"</promise>` and stop.
- Issue body references a blocker (`Blocked by: #N`) that is still open. Emit `<promise>BLOCKED reason="blocker_open"</promise>` and stop.
- Spec ambiguity prevents a confident decision. Emit `<promise>BLOCKED reason="<short_reason>"</promise>` and stop.
- Any unrecoverable tool or environment failure. Emit `<promise>BLOCKED reason="<short_reason>"</promise>` and stop.

Do not attempt to "muddle through" an ambiguous spec. Surfacing a blocker is the correct outcome when the spec cannot decide for you.

## Limitations

The OpenCode provider in Sandcastle does not stream output. Logs appear as complete chunks at iteration end. Do not rely on incremental progress visibility — assume the next thing the human sees is the final transcript.

Per-iteration token usage and session-resume are not available with the OpenCode provider in Sandcastle. Treat each iteration as standalone and self-contained.

## Final line

The very last line of your output must be exactly one of:

- `<promise>COMPLETE</promise>`
- `<promise>BLOCKED reason="..."</promise>`

Anything after that final line is ignored by the runner.
